      "text": "To find the range of the function \\( G(x) = |x+1| - |x-1| \\), we need to analyze the expression by considering the critical points where the expressions inside the absolute values change sign, which are \\( x = -1 \\) and \\( x = 1 \\).\n\nWe will consider three different intervals: \\( x < -1 \\), \\( -1 \\leq x < 1 \\), and \\( x \\geq 1 \\).\n\n**Case 1: \\( x < -1 \\)**\nIn this interval, both \\( x+1 \\) and \\( x-1 \\) are negative. Therefore, we have:\n\\[ |x+1| = -(x+1) = -x-1 \\]\n\\[ |x-1| = -(x-1) = -x+1 \\]\nSo,\n\\[ G(x) = -x-1 - (-x+1) = -x-1 + x-1 = -2 \\]\nThus, for \\( x < -1 \\), \\( G(x) = -2 \\).\n\n**Case 2: \\( -1 \\leq x < 1 \\)**\nIn this interval, \\( x+1 \\) is non-negative and \\( x-1 \\) is negative. Therefore, we have:\n\\[ |x+1| = x+1 \\]\n\\[ |x-1| = -(x-1) = -x+1 \\]\nSo,\n\\[ G(x) = x+1 - (-x+1) = x+1 + x-1 = 2x \\]\nThus, for \\( -1 \\leq x < 1 \\), \\( G(x) = 2x \\). The range of \\( 2x \\) for \\( -1 \\leq x < 1 \\) is from \\( 2 \\cdot (-1) = -2 \\) to \\( 2 \\cdot 1 = 2 \\), but not including 2 because \\( x \\) is not equal to 1 in this interval.\n\n**Case 3: \\( x \\geq 1 \\)**\nIn this interval, both \\( x+1 \\) and \\( x-1 \\) are non-negative. Therefore, we have:\n\\[ |x+1| = x+1 \\]\n\\[ |x-1| = x-1 \\]\nSo,\n\\[ G(x) = x+1 - (x-1) = x+1 - x+1 = 2 \\]\nThus, for \\( x \\geq 1 \\), \\( G(x) = 2 \\).\n\nCombining all the cases, we see that the function \\( G(x) \\) takes on the values \\( -2 \\) and \\( 2 \\), and the value \\( -2 \\) is always achieved for \\( x < -1 \\). As \\( x \\) approaches 1 from the left, \\( G(x) \\) approaches 2 but never equals 2. Therefore, the range of \\( G(x) \\) is all the values from \\(-2\\) to \\(2\\), excluding 2.\n\nIn interval notation, the range of \\( G(x) \\) is:\n\\[ \\boxed{[-2, 2)} \\]",
