      "text": "To determine the range of the function \\( G(x) = |x+1| - |x-1| \\), we need to analyze the expression in different intervals based on the points where the absolute value expressions change, which are at \\( x = -1 \\) and \\( x = 1 \\).\n\nWe will consider three intervals: \\( x < -1 \\), \\( -1 \\leq x < 1 \\), and \\( x \\geq 1 \\).\n\n### Interval 1: \\( x < -1 \\)\nFor \\( x < -1 \\), both \\( x+1 \\) and \\( x-1 \\) are negative. Therefore, we can rewrite the function as:\n\\[ G(x) = -(x+1) - (-(x-1)) = -x-1 + x-1 = -2. \\]\nSo, for \\( x < -1 \\), \\( G(x) = -2 \\).\n\n### Interval 2: \\( -1 \\leq x < 1 \\)\nFor \\( -1 \\leq x < 1 \\), \\( x+1 \\) is non-negative and \\( x-1 \\) is negative. Therefore, we can rewrite the function as:\n\\[ G(x) = (x+1) - (-(x-1)) = x+1 + x-1 = 2x. \\]\nSo, for \\( -1 \\leq x < 1 \\), \\( G(x) = 2x \\). The value of \\( G(x) \\) ranges from \\( G(-1) = 2(-1) = -2 \\) to \\( G(1) = 2(1) = 2 \\).\n\n### Interval 3: \\( x \\geq 1 \\)\nFor \\( x \\geq 1 \\), both \\( x+1 \\) and \\( x-1 \\) are non-negative. Therefore, we can rewrite the function as:\n\\[ G(x) = (x+1) - (x-1) = x+1 - x+1 = 2. \\]\nSo, for \\( x \\geq 1 \\), \\( G(x) = 2 \\).\n\nCombining the results from all three intervals, we see that the function \\( G(x) \\) takes on all values from \\(-2\\) to \\(2\\), including the value \\(2\\) at \\( x = 1 \\) and the value \\(-2\\) at \\( x = -1 \\), but does not take on the value \\(0\\).\n\nTherefore, the range of the function \\( G(x) = |x+1| - |x-1| \\) is \\(\\boxed{[-2, 2]}\\).",
