    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To find the range of the function \\(G(x) = |x+1| - |x-1|\\), we need to analyze the behavior of the function by considering different cases based on the critical points where the expressions inside the absolute values change sign. These critical points are \\(x = -1\\) and \\(x = 1\\).\n\nWe will break down the function into three cases based on the value of \\(x\\):\n\n1. **Case 1: \\(x < -1\\)**\n\n   In this interval, both \\(x+1 < 0\\) and \\(x-1 < 0\\). Therefore, we have:\n   \\[\n   G(x) = -(x+1) - (-(x-1)) = -x-1 + x-1 = -2\n   \\]\n   So, for \\(x < -1\\), \\(G(x) = -2\\).\n\n2. **Case 2: \\(-1 \\leq x < 1\\)**\n\n   In this interval, \\(x+1 \\geq 0\\) and \\(x-1 < 0\\). Therefore, we have:\n   \\[\n   G(x) = (x+1) - (-(x-1)) = x+1 + x-1 = 2x\n   \\]\n   So, for \\(-1 \\leq x < 1\\), \\(G(x) = 2x\\). The function \\(G(x) = 2x\\) maps the interval \\([-1, 1)\\) to the interval \\([-2, 2)\\).\n\n3. **Case 3: \\(x \\geq 1\\)**\n\n   In this interval, both \\(x+1 \\geq 0\\) and \\(x-1 \\geq 0\\). Therefore, we have:\n   \\[\n   G(x) = (x+1) - (x-1) = x+1 - x+1 = 2\n   \\]\n   So, for \\(x \\geq 1\\), \\(G(x) = 2\\).\n\nNow, we need to combine the results from the three cases. The function \\(G(x)\\) takes the values \\(-2\\) for \\(x < -1\\), \\([-2, 2)\\) for \\(-1 \\leq x < 1\\), and \\(2\\) for \\(x \\geq 1\\). Therefore, the range of \\(G(x)\\) is all real numbers from \\(-2\\) to \\(2\\), inclusive.\n\nThus, the range of the function \\(G(x) = |x+1| - |x-1|\\) is \\(\\boxed{[-2, 2]}\\).",
